WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.367
Strong detection rules often begin with a behavioral question.

00:00:03.367 --> 00:00:05.907
What activity are we trying to identify?

00:00:05.907 --> 00:00:07.722
What suspicious behavior concerns us?

00:00:07.722 --> 00:00:12.440
What risk would the organization face if this behavior occurred and went unnoticed?

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.344
Behavior-first thinking helps teams avoid random rule creation.

00:00:15.344 --> 00:00:20.062
Instead of starting with a log field and looking for something to match,

00:00:20.062 --> 00:00:25.870
detection engineers start with a meaningful defensive objective and then determine what evidence could support it.

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:29.499
Useful inputs include threat intelligence, prior incidents, ATT&CK technique mapping,

00:00:29.499 --> 00:00:32.040
organizational priorities, red team lessons, compliance concerns,

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.581
and patterns analysts have observed during investigations.

00:00:34.581 --> 00:00:38.573
These inputs help keep the rule connected to real operational need.

00:00:38.573 --> 00:00:39.299
Context matters.

00:00:39.299 --> 00:00:44.017
The same behavior may be normal in one environment and suspicious in another.

00:00:44.017 --> 00:00:48.373
A good detection idea considers who performs the activity, where it happens,

00:00:48.373 --> 00:00:50.188
how often it is expected,

00:00:50.188 --> 00:00:54.180
and what surrounding events would make it more or less concerning.
